WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:05.669
Whilst we could roll our own solution for input by polling for keys every frame, Unreal

2
00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:09.929
provides a fantastic and flexible way of adding inputs to our game with full keyboard, mouse,

3
00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:13.470
controller, joystick, touch and VR support.

4
00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:16.949
Unreal supports two systems when it comes to handling input.

5
00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:20.109
There's the old, what we're going to call legacy input support that is rooted

6
00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:21.670
heavily in Unreal 4.

7
00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:25.980
And then there's the newer, more flexible and feature-rich enhanced input.

8
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.260
The legacy system offers a system that can handle hardware inputs, be it from keyboard

9
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.460
and mouse, console controllers and touch controls.

10
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:37.500
Specialised input devices that don't conform to the aforementioned inputs can also be handled

11
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.530
through the raw input plugin.

12
00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:44.729
The player input is a U object that resides within the player controller, and it's tasked

13
00:00:44.729 --> 00:00:46.450
with managing the player's input.

14
00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:50.369
It's also important to note that this object is only spawned on the client, so we have

15
00:00:50.369 --> 00:00:54.070
no server-side input.

16
00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:58.490
Within Unreal, we can make use of two modes of input binding.

17
00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:01.000
The first is an action mapping.

18
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.519
These will map a button or key pressed to a friendly name that will later be bound to

19
00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:06.359
gameplay behaviours.

20
00:01:06.359 --> 00:01:10.000
This is not polled, rather the gameplay behaviour linked to this mapping will fire

21
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.799
when the button in question is pressed or released.

22
00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:16.000
Secondly, we've got axis mappings.

23
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.260
These will map a button or key pressed to a friendly name that will later be bound to

24
00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:23.340
a continuous gameplay behaviour, such as player movement.

25
00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.219
These mappings are polled continuously, even when they're not being pressed, in which case

26
00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:31.500
they're going to return a value of zero.

27
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.019
Input mapping can be done both in native, as we can see at the top of the slide, or

28
00:01:35.019 --> 00:01:39.799
in the Unreal Engine editor, as we can see at the bottom of the slide.

29
00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:43.319
Whilst within the editor, we can access the input binding mappings through the project

30
00:01:43.319 --> 00:01:45.780
settings menu.

31
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.439
Under the engine menu header on the left, we can select input, which will then take

32
00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:53.060
us to the window that we can see at the bottom of this slide.

33
00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:57.319
Input binding in Unreal is simple and can be done in both native C++ and blueprint,

34
00:01:57.319 --> 00:02:01.939
giving developers flexibility in how they implement this functionality.

35
00:02:02.019 --> 00:02:08.189
Next we can look at a simple native C++ implementation of binding to our input in our gameplay code.

36
00:02:08.189 --> 00:02:12.590
As we can see in the first image, we simply override the setupPlayerInputComponent function,

37
00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:17.340
which passes through the input component for the character in this example.

38
00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.419
To bind an action, we simply feed through the action name, in our case jump, and

39
00:02:21.419 --> 00:02:26.210
then how we want to react to this event either on the key pressed or on the key released.

40
00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:29.289
Next we pass through the object that the function will be called on, and then lastly

41
00:02:29.289 --> 00:02:32.979
we pass through a function pointer that will be called directly.

42
00:02:32.979 --> 00:02:37.300
For an axis binding, again we feed through an action name, in our case moveForward.

43
00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.919
Then we pass through the object that we want to call this function on, and lastly the

44
00:02:39.919 --> 00:02:42.080
function pointer.

45
00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.020
Because an axis binding is continuously polled, we don't need to pass through a pressed

46
00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.580
or released parameter, it's going to be polled regardless.

47
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.750
And now for the blueprint implementation, and as we can see, it is much much more simple.

48
00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:59.389
Having created our input mappings as shown on the previous slide, Unreal will create

49
00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:05.550
handy InputAxis and InputAction events that match the name of our input mappings.

50
00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:09.069
In the top node, we have our continuously polled axis mapping, which feeds through the

51
00:03:09.069 --> 00:03:12.430
value detected by the input system.

52
00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:16.210
We still need to keep in mind that even though a button is not pressed, it will continue

53
00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:20.530
to feed through a value of 0 to any of these bindings.

54
00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:24.379
We can see on the bottom node that we have our action mappings.

55
00:03:24.379 --> 00:03:28.180
Blueprint allows us to use either the pressed and or released callbacks to bind our gameplay

56
00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:33.139
functionality, as well as feed through the key that was detected.

57
00:03:33.139 --> 00:03:36.379
So now that we know how input mapping and input binding works, we should maybe take

58
00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:41.060
a look at how the actual input is processed under the hood.

59
00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:45.620
The Enhanced Input System is a plugin that makes creating and manipulating inputs easier

60
00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.629
by means of making them asset based.

61
00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:51.870
There are two types of asset we need to be familiar with when working with the Enhanced

62
00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:53.530
Input System.

63
00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:58.229
The first is the InputAction, and this defines the concept of a binding.

64
00:03:58.229 --> 00:04:02.740
For example, we might create a new asset called WalkForwards or Jump.

65
00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:07.259
We've not actually linked the idea to a set of keys or modifiers, merely outlined that

66
00:04:07.259 --> 00:04:11.360
walking forwards and jumping is something that we want to be able to do in our game

67
00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.110
and bind gameplay functionality to it.

68
00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:18.629
In this sense, we can think of an InputAction as a way of identifying a particular section

69
00:04:18.629 --> 00:04:21.930
of the keyboard bindings later down the line.

70
00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:27.170
The InputContext lets us marry that InputAction to a set of hardware inputs such as keys

71
00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:30.000
to be used in our gameplay code.

72
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.920
The InputAction asset is actually fairly sparse in terms of settings and parameters

73
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:34.920
for us to play with.

74
00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:39.300
The most important parameter we should look at is the value type where we specify what

75
00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.329
kind of data we expect this input to produce.

76
00:04:43.329 --> 00:04:49.360
Digital is a 0 or a 1, ideal for fire and forget actions such as jumping.

77
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.420
Axis 1 is ideal for strength based inputs like move forwards.

78
00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:59.259
Axis 2 is good for bi-directional inputs such as thumbstick input, assessing both X

79
00:04:59.259 --> 00:05:02.089
and Y at the same time.

80
00:05:02.089 --> 00:05:05.889
Axis 3 is good for things like spatial movements or inputs that take into account

81
00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:09.550
a vertical movement like VR input.

82
00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:13.930
As mentioned earlier in this topic, an InputContext gives us a method of linking input actions

83
00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:16.189
to keys and behaviors.

84
00:05:16.189 --> 00:05:19.970
In terms of actual setup, we can see we simply choose what input action we want to

85
00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:25.389
add to our context before defining what hardware inputs we want to link to it.

86
00:05:25.389 --> 00:05:29.689
You can also see that we have an array of modifiers per key, and this is fantastically

87
00:05:29.689 --> 00:05:32.790
useful when dealing with things like dead zones.

88
00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:36.769
We can simply add a dead zone modifier to our thumbstick binding, and Unreal will

89
00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:40.470
handle filtering out the dead zone input for us, so no need to build in any additional

90
00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:44.189
functionality to our code to handle these errant cases.

91
00:05:44.189 --> 00:05:47.470
Of course, we're free to author our own modifiers to complement the ones that come

92
00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.850
out of the box.

93
00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:55.050
Binding to an enhanced input is similar to our legacy bindings but with a couple more

94
00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:56.209
steps.

95
00:05:56.209 --> 00:06:01.050
Firstly, we need to push our context, and this is our opportunity to source the correct

96
00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:03.910
context for our current circumstances.

97
00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:08.569
Do we need the swimming context, or the walking context, or even the driving a car

98
00:06:08.569 --> 00:06:09.910
context?

99
00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:12.720
We simply add the context that we need.

100
00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.199
And of course, we're free to clear all mappings as shown above.

101
00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:19.180
Lastly, we need to bind our actions.

102
00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:24.319
In this case, InputMove is an input action we've defined alongside our contexts.

103
00:06:24.319 --> 00:06:28.259
You'll notice that the IE pressed and IE released enums have been replaced with an

104
00:06:28.259 --> 00:06:33.060
E-trigger event enum, covering an extended spread of input cases.

105
00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:40.420
We can now support triggered, started, ongoing, cancelled, and completed as trigger types.

106
00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:44.319
Input components are present on all actors, but are most commonly seen inside pawns and

107
00:06:44.319 --> 00:06:46.060
player controllers.

108
00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:49.819
They can be added to level script actors as well, as the level is, after all, an

109
00:06:49.819 --> 00:06:52.420
actor in its own right.

110
00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:56.199
The input component links the input mappings that we've just made to the actions that

111
00:06:56.199 --> 00:07:01.459
we wish to see in our game, and this can be done both in native or in Blueprint.

112
00:07:01.459 --> 00:07:05.149
Input processing in Unreal works from a stack.

113
00:07:05.149 --> 00:07:07.839
The priority stack for input handling can be seen here.

114
00:07:07.839 --> 00:07:12.139
Firstly, it handles any actors that have except input enabled, from the most recently

115
00:07:12.139 --> 00:07:15.819
enabled to the least recently enabled.

116
00:07:15.819 --> 00:07:18.939
Controllers are handled next, followed by the level blueprint, and lastly, we deal

117
00:07:18.939 --> 00:07:21.160
with pawns.

118
00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:25.800
If one input component consumes the input, then the input is not available further down

119
00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.279
the input stack.

120
00:07:28.279 --> 00:07:32.259
As a general tip, if you wish for a particular actor to always be considered ahead of the

121
00:07:32.259 --> 00:07:38.120
others, re-enabling its except input will move it to the top of this stack.

122
00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:42.759
Lastly, for projects that require more complex input features like complex input handling

123
00:07:42.759 --> 00:07:47.720
or runtime control remapping, the enhanced input plugin implements such features as

124
00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:55.480
radial dead zones, chorded actions, contextual input and prioritization, and finally it will

125
00:07:55.480 --> 00:08:02.259
extend the bespoke filtering and processing of raw input data in an asset-based environment.

126
00:08:02.259 --> 00:08:04.519
Let's talk about delegates and events.

127
00:08:04.519 --> 00:08:08.439
Unreal provides a full suite of delegates and event macros for us to leverage into

128
00:08:08.439 --> 00:08:13.199
performant sensibly structured code architectures, eliminating the need for constant condition

129
00:08:13.199 --> 00:08:14.360
polling.

130
00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:19.670
So let's take a look at what delegates are in Unreal and how we can go about declaring them.

131
00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:22.420
So quick recap, what is a delegate?

132
00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:27.220
A delegate is a data type that can reference and execute member functions on C++ objects

133
00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:31.209
in a generic and type safe way.

134
00:08:31.209 --> 00:08:34.470
Delegates can be bound dynamically to a member function of an object to call that

135
00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:37.000
object in the future.

136
00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:40.639
Delegates are safe to copy and can be passed around by value, however this process

137
00:08:40.639 --> 00:08:44.799
will allocate memory on the heap, so instead it's highly recommended that you pass delegates

138
00:08:44.799 --> 00:08:47.909
by reference whenever possible.

139
00:08:47.909 --> 00:08:52.720
So let's take a look at what types of delegates Unreal actually offers.

140
00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:56.750
The Unreal Engine offers the following types of delegates.

141
00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:59.789
Firstly we have single delegates, and this is a one-to-one binding, and they can

142
00:08:59.789 --> 00:09:02.490
be saved and loaded.

143
00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:05.889
Next are multicast delegates, and this is a one-to-many binding, and these delegates

144
00:09:05.889 --> 00:09:09.590
can be bound by both reflection types and raw lambdas.

145
00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:15.320
It's important to note that multicast delegates cannot use return values.

146
00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:19.159
Next we have dynamic delegates, and these again are a one-to-many binding, however these

147
00:09:19.159 --> 00:09:23.470
delegates can only be bound by reflection types and are serializable.

148
00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:26.629
It's also important to note that these delegates are slower than regular delegates,

149
00:09:26.629 --> 00:09:30.649
and execution of a dynamic delegate must be paired with an isBound check prior to

150
00:09:30.649 --> 00:09:33.039
calling execute.

151
00:09:33.039 --> 00:09:37.759
Alternatively, we can make use of the executeIfBound function, which will both check if it

152
00:09:37.759 --> 00:09:41.440
is bound and then execute for us.

153
00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:44.440
Lastly we have events.

154
00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:47.679
Events offer the same functionality as a multicast delegate.

155
00:09:47.679 --> 00:09:53.519
However the broadcast functionality is retained exclusively by the class that declares the event.

156
00:09:53.519 --> 00:09:57.240
Derived classes of the base class which declared this event in the first place do

157
00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:00.639
not inherit access to that broadcast.

158
00:10:00.639 --> 00:10:04.820
If a derived class wishes to make use of that functionality, then the base class

159
00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:10.820
must expose some kind of protected function that will fire the broadcast for them.

160
00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:14.440
Lastly we do have abstract events which can be inherited by derived classes, but we'll

161
00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:18.580
explain more about those in the next few slides.

162
00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:23.779
Unreal offers a wide range of macros that can be used to declare a delegate or event.

163
00:10:23.779 --> 00:10:27.379
Each macro features parameters for your new delegate, as well as the function's return

164
00:10:27.379 --> 00:10:31.159
type, however the declaration of this delegate differs from types that should simply

165
00:10:31.159 --> 00:10:33.639
return void.

166
00:10:33.639 --> 00:10:36.039
Delegate macros support the following combinations.

167
00:10:36.039 --> 00:10:40.500
We have functions that return a value, functions that are declared as const, and

168
00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:45.870
then we have up to four payload variables as well as eight function parameters.

169
00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:49.269
To declare a delegate, we simply use the corresponding macro for our particular technical

170
00:10:49.269 --> 00:10:51.039
design.

171
00:10:51.039 --> 00:10:54.600
For example, in the top image we declare a dynamic multicast delegate that passes through

172
00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.960
a float payload to the object that is bound to it.

173
00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:02.080
And as you may have noticed, this example is accompanied by the uDelegate macro.

174
00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:06.080
Delegate functions support the same specifiers as u functions, but you need to use the

175
00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:08.889
uDelegate macro instead.

176
00:11:08.889 --> 00:11:12.370
In the second example, we declare an event that is owned by the adventure component

177
00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:16.240
and passes through a float payload to the object binding to it.

178
00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:19.519
In the third example, we declare a delegate that returns a float and passes through an

179
00:11:19.559 --> 00:11:22.850
f-string payload to the object that is bound to it.

180
00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:26.929
Lastly, in the last two images, we can see the declaration of an abstract event in the

181
00:11:26.929 --> 00:11:28.850
inventory interface.

182
00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:32.450
This event passes through a float payload to the object that is bound to it, and then

183
00:11:32.450 --> 00:11:37.610
in a derived class, in this case an inventory component, we declare a derived event that

184
00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:41.009
is owned by the component instead, and thus can be fired by it.
